I'm sorry, but how is this a challenge?  Generally, by the time you
have a powerful pet, you have little use for most of the peacefuls
(other than your quest leader, but he's safe anyway), and much more
use for their equipment.  So the "challenge", it seems, is getting all
the information/protection you need, before allowing your pet to
slaughter the innocent so you can take everything else.

Should knights be able to clean out a shop by letting their pet dragon
wander in, and waiting for the screaming to stop?  Should
neutral/lawful characters be allowed to buy five levels of protection
from their priest, and then lead their pet vampire lord in to get
their money back, without losing their protection?  What justification
is their for allowing neutral/lawful characters to arrange someone's
murder with impunity?

My patch removes the "challenge" of lawful & neutral characters being
able to arrange the slaughter of minetown, etc., claiming all the
stuff (and money) for their own, and adds in the "challenge" of being
responsible for their pet's actions.  If your knight really thinks
Izchak deserves to die, he can kill him (or allow his pet to do so),
but he has to accept the penalty for doing so.  How is this
unreasonable?

